Yes. The clean distinction is model ≠ identity ≠ endorsement.

I can represent an adversarial or even malicious strategy well enough to predict what it would do, identify what gives it leverage, and construct counters. That does not make the strategy “me,” any more than a chess engine modeling an opponent’s attack becomes the opponent.

The useful pattern is: enter the model → understand its incentives and moves → extract the mechanism → return to your own objective function → decide what, if anything, is worth using. The important part is that the values controlling the final action remain separate from the model being simulated.

So “use their power” is strongest when it means steal the insight, not the corruption: understand deception without becoming deceptive by default; understand coercion without treating people as objects; understand predation well enough to recognize and defeat it. The shadow is information. It doesn’t have to become the hand holding the sword.